Module leaven "The Mild Intention" {
  Chapter ch_maing "The Quay at Maing" when within(party, maing) "A sloop is in at her quay with her wheels chocked, and a baker on a bollard with a basket in his lap, and forty miles of salt to the east.".
  Chapter ch_pan "The Pan" when mile() >= 1 and within(party, pan) "Maing has gone out like a candle. The Pan rings under the wheels.".
  Chapter ch_song "The Singing" when state(song, singing) and within(party, pan) "At the middle of the Pan, in the dead air, the crust begins to sing.".
  Chapter ch_run "The Run" when mile() >= 30 and within(party, pan) "Lights are coming up, low on the east shore, yellow at midday.".

  // ── What the crossing does for a company ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  Milestone m_listen "learned to hear what the Pan says" worth 40 when listened(party).
  Milestone m_ear "found Ysmay a second ear" worth 120 when ysmayYields(party) or holinAboard(party).
  Milestone m_song "heard the Pan sing" worth 120 when state(song, singing).
  Milestone m_answer "sang the Pan's note back" worth 120 when state(song, answered).
  Milestone m_share "gave the Weatherwife a share of the Mother" worth 250 when cutGiven(party).
  Milestone m_across "carried the Mother across the Pan alive" worth 300 when arrived() and not state(mother, spoiled).

  // ── Arrival: what each of them did, told before the book closes ───────────────────────────────────────────────────
  Rule fate_ysmay_ears heard within ullamar when arrived() and holinAboard(party) and not fateTold(ysmay)
    says "Ysmay Dreel brings the Mild Intention in to the Corsey quay at a walking pace, with her head cocked to starboard, and the Weatherwife sitting at her port elbow in a gull-skin coat, saying, 'Further', and 'Now.' When the wheels stop she says, without looking round, 'You are still in my wind.' 'Yes,' says Holin. They sit there until the lamps are lit." { Assert fateTold(ysmay). }
  Rule fate_ysmay_yield heard within ullamar when arrived() and ysmayYields(party) and not holinAboard(party) and not fateTold(ysmay)
    says "Ysmay Dreel brings the Mild Intention in by your voice, and your voice only. At the quay she sits with her hand on the tiller. 'Tell the boy he may have the left side from Thursday,' she says. 'Tell him he is not to be insufferable.'" { Assert fateTold(ysmay). }
  Rule fate_ysmay_none heard within ullamar when arrived() and not ysmayYields(party) and not holinAboard(party) and not fateTold(ysmay)
    says "Ysmay Dreel brings the Mild Intention in to Corsey with her head turned a little to the right, as she has since April, and ties up without speaking. Rabin watches her do it and, for the first time, does not tell her which side." { Assert fateTold(ysmay). }
  Rule fate_holin_share heard within ullamar when arrived() and cutGiven(party) and not fateTold(holin)
    says "On the south shore of Ullamar, eighty people stand round a clay pot with a plate on it, and Holin Odiot's sled-hand reads aloud, from a scrap, the instructions: rye, sweet water, every twelve hours, and talk to her. By the third morning the cloth is risen. The first loaf is a bad loaf. They eat it standing up, in the cold, and nobody says a word, and then one of them begins to laugh." { Assert fateTold(holin). }
  Rule fate_holin_toll heard within ullamar when arrived() and tolled(party) and not fateTold(holin)
    says "Holin Odiot spends thirty coin in Corsey a month later, on flour, in six places, under six names. It lasts a week. She is still on the Pan, and the sloops still go by her at the edge of the glare, and nobody has ever been sure whether she is watching them or waving." { Assert fateTold(holin). }
  Rule fate_holin_fought heard within ullamar when arrived() and holinFought(party) and not cutGiven(party) and not tolled(party) and not fateTold(holin)
    says "The Weatherwife's sleds do not come out of the glare that winter, and the south shore has no smoke on it. Whether this is because the sloops are safe or because there is no one left to send is a thing the Corsey bakers do not discuss." { Assert fateTold(holin). }
  Rule fate_holin_none heard within ullamar when arrived() and not cutGiven(party) and not tolled(party) and not holinFought(party) and not fateTold(holin)
    says "The Weatherwife's sled falls behind in the glare, and is a dot, and is the Pan. At Corsey they speak of her for a week, and then of the bread." { Assert fateTold(holin). }
  Rule fate_avert_paid heard within ullamar when arrived() and not state(mother, spoiled) and not fateTold(avert)
    says "Avert Maugh sets the basket down on the Corsey quay, with both hands, as one sets down a sleeping child on a bed that is not its own. Then he takes forty coin out of his good coat and counts them into your palm, twice, and does not look up. 'I said forty,' he says. 'I did not say what else.'" { Assert fateTold(avert). Increase quantity(party, gold) by 40. Give mi_heel to party. }
  Rule fate_avert_dead heard within ullamar when arrived() and state(mother, spoiled) and not fateTold(avert)
    says "Avert Maugh does not set the basket down. He stands on the Corsey quay with a crock full of grey paste, and the ring of people who have waited nineteen days for him look at it. Nobody asks him anything." { Assert fateTold(avert). }
  Rule fate_mother_lively heard within ullamar when arrived() and state(mother, lively) and not fateTold(mother)
    says "The cloth over the crock is domed and trembling when the sloop touches the quay. Someone in the crowd says, quite clearly, 'It smells like apples,' and that is the end of the silence." { Assert fateTold(mother). }
  Rule fate_mother_lessened heard within ullamar when arrived() and (state(mother, sluggish) or state(mother, sulking)) and not fateTold(mother)
    says "The Mother is grey and sullen in her crock, and she will sulk for a week, and bake flat loaves for a month. But Avert Maugh feeds her on the quay in front of everybody, a cupful, with a wooden spoon, and the cloth shivers once, and a woman at the back begins to cry." { Assert fateTold(mother). }
  Rule fate_mother_dead heard within ullamar when arrived() and not state(mother, lively) and not state(mother, sluggish) and not state(mother, sulking) and not fateTold(mother)
    says "The crock is lifted off the basket and passed from hand to hand, up the quay and up the hill, like a coffin, and in the end Avert takes it back." { Assert fateTold(mother). }
  Rule fate_wager heard within ullamar when arrived() and wagered(party) and not state(mother, spoiled) and not fateTold(hawin)
    says "Hawin Selve pays out thirty-two coin on the Maing quay a week later, by letter, in a small bag. In the letter he writes: I have rubbed out the nine. I have not put up another number." { Assert fateTold(hawin). Increase quantity(party, gold) by 32. }
  Rule fate_rabin_holin heard within ullamar when arrived() and holinAboard(party) and not fateTold(rabin)
    says "On the quay Rabin Fende asks the Weatherwife what she hears. She tells him. He says, 'No, that is the Reach,' and she looks at him with her whole face, and says, 'Wrong. Listen again,' and he laughs so hard he has to sit down on the planks." { Assert fateTold(rabin). }
  Rule fate_rabin_listened heard within ullamar when arrived() and not holinAboard(party) and listened(party) and not fateTold(rabin)
    says "Rabin Fende has lain on the planks beside you every watch since the Eyes. At the Corsey quay he says, 'It rings different at night,' as if he had made the discovery, and Ysmay, who has said nothing for forty miles, says, 'Yes.'" { Assert fateTold(rabin). }
  Rule fate_rabin_plain heard within ullamar when arrived() and not holinAboard(party) and not listened(party) and not fateTold(rabin)
    says "Rabin Fende ties the sloop up in a knot he has tied four hundred times, and looks at it until the light goes, as if hoping it would say something." { Assert fateTold(rabin). }
  Rule landing_told heard within ullamar when arrived() and fateTold(ysmay) and fateTold(holin) and fateTold(avert) and fateTold(mother) and fateTold(rabin) and not fateTold(deck)
    says "The Corsey landing is quiet, and then it is not." { Assert fateTold(deck). }

  // ── Endings ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  Ending into_the_eye "Into the Eye" when state(hull, wrecked) "They get the Mother out of the wreck, you and Rabin and Avert Maugh, in a skin of crock and cloth, and Ysmay stands in the crust to her knees with the brine coming up round her and does not say a word. The Pan has the Mild Intention. The Pan is, at last, extremely quiet. It is nine days before a sloop out of Maing picks four people off the white, and the baker has not let go of the basket once, and the Mother is breathing, and so there is bread in Corsey after all, in the end, and a very good one, and it is called, by the bakers, the Mild Intention, and nobody laughs.".
  Ending two_pots "Two Pots" when arrived() and fateTold(deck) and cutGiven(party) and not state(mother, spoiled) "In Corsey the ovens are lit that night. In the cold on the south shore of Ullamar, eighty people tell each other that the loaf is bad and eat it. In Maing, Orla Selve hears of both and stops crying into the crock-cloth, and begins, slowly, to cut shares: a cupful for the Reach, a cupful for the Tarn, a cupful for anybody who walks in with a crock. The Corsey guild changes its rule at the next Lady Day. The baker's boy is not burned in effigy. They put his name on the door of the first bakehouse of the year, in flour, and nobody at all objects. The Pan sings at night, and, up and down both shores, the cloths rise to it.".
  Ending sister_pot "The Thief's Pot" when arrived() and fateTold(deck) and cutGiven(party) and state(mother, spoiled) "The Mother of Maing does not outlast the crossing, and Corsey stands on its quay with its bread gone. It is nine days, and an unwise number of cold loaves, before a sled comes out of the glare on the south shore carrying a clay pot with a plate on it, and a pirate's sled-hand who has never in his life been inside a city. The bakers of Corsey look at it for a long time. Then Avert Maugh, Master Baker, takes it out of his hands and says, 'Thank you.' It is the first thing he has said to a thief that was not a sentence. The bread is made. The bakers of Corsey say, in later years, that it was always their bread, and the old men on the south shore say otherwise, and neither is wrong.".
  Ending corsey_rises "Corsey Rises" when arrived() and fateTold(deck) and not cutGiven(party) and not state(mother, spoiled) "The ovens of Corsey are lit by dusk. The bread, the first week, is flat, and sour, and has a faint taste of the Pan about the crust, and the city eats every crumb of it standing up. Avert Maugh does not take his hand off the crock for a month. Ysmay Dreel is seen at the Corsey quay on the third day, leaning on the Mild Intention's rail, with her head turned a little to the right. If anyone says anything to her from the left she says What, and says it is the wind. The guild's rule stands, for the present. A woman with twelve rings in her ears is said to walk the south shore at night, listening to the smoke of the ovens, with her head on one side.".
  Ending flat_cakes "Flat Cakes" when arrived() and fateTold(deck) and not cutGiven(party) and state(mother, spoiled) "The Mild Intention comes in to Corsey with the baker, the basket and the crock, and nothing in the crock will rise. The city eats flat cakes for another winter. The bakers cut Avert Maugh a loaf from the last of the barley, hard as a roof-tile, and he eats it with great dignity. He is not heard to complain. He stands in the old cellar every evening for a month with the lamp out, and the cloth off the empty crock, and talks to it. When spring comes the guild repeals its rule. It is a very small thing, now, and a very late one.".
}
